WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.020
Good morning. Hank. It's Tuesday.

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:03.160
So I'm on vacation on the shores of Lake Michigan

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:05.760
and last night Twilight looked like this.

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.640
I learned yesterday that the first half hour or so after the sun sets

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:11.520
is called Civil Twilight, because it's considered by some

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.460
governments to be legally still daytime.

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:15.180
Like centuries ago,

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:19.040
nighttime burglary was often considered a more serious crime than daytime burglary,

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:21.520
and criminals would sometimes seek lesser sentences

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:25.440
by claiming that they robbed a house not at night, but during Civil Twilight.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.540
I just love that phrase Civil twilight,

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:31.000
the adjective Civil makes almost every noun better except war.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:31.500
Anyway,

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.240
I've been thinking a lot about sunsets this week

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:34.420
because the ones here in Michigan

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:37.500
are so ostentatiously beautiful, like back home in Indiana

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:41.069
The light often gets gray and then grayer until it's dark.

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:45.389
But here in Michigan, at least at this time of year these sunsets are mundane

00:00:45.489 --> 00:00:48.479
They're ordinary which also makes them cliche of course

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.660
I mean the beauty of sunsets and the Civil Twilight's that follow is by now

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:58.770
So cliche that it's difficult to say anything interesting about them like I love E. E. Cummings famous sunset poem which goes: who are you little

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:04.830
eye, 5 or 6 years old peering from some high window at the gold of November Sunset and

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:09.449
Feeling that if day has to become night. This is a beautiful way

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:10.500
It's a good poem

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:16.919
But it only works because Cummings situates the observation in childhood when one is presumably too innocent to have yet realized

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:21.600
How lame it is to write about sunsets and yet a good sunset is beautiful and better still

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:27.419
universally so like our ancestors didn't eat like us or travel like us their relationship to ideas as

00:01:27.549 --> 00:01:30.868
Fundamental as light and time were vastly different from ours

00:01:30.869 --> 00:01:36.599
but every human who's lived more than a Few years on this planet has seen a beautiful sunset and paused to spend one of the

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:43.619
Last moments of day to be grateful for and overwhelmed by the light, but even now I'm swimming in sentimental waters

00:01:43.619 --> 00:01:46.258
How do you celebrate a sunset without being cheesy maybe

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:51.779
stated in cold facts? So here's what happens before a beam of sunlight gets to your eyes it has millions of

00:01:51.909 --> 00:01:53.909
interactions with molecules that cause the so-called

00:01:54.220 --> 00:02:00.449
Scattering of light different wavelengths are sent off to different directions when interacting with say oxygen or nitrogen in the atmosphere

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:02.450
But at sunset the light travels through the atmosphere

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:07.740
Longer before it reaches our eyes so that much of the blue and purple has been scattered away before it reaches us

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:09.599
I think it's helpful to know how sunsets work

00:02:09.599 --> 00:02:14.578
And I've never bought the romantic notion that scientific understanding somehow robs the universe of its beauty

00:02:14.579 --> 00:02:19.349
But it still doesn't communicate much about how I felt looking at this absolutely routine

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:21.839
Totally un-extraordinary Civil Twilight

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:24.809
How it felt to look at something that was as beautiful as it was

00:02:24.879 --> 00:02:31.408
Normal and to be comforted both by the beauty and the normalcy so anyway I tried to photograph it because you know that's what people

00:02:31.409 --> 00:02:36.269
do but of course the camera couldn't capture how I felt in the space between yesterday and last night and

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:40.739
Apparently neither can my voice over. All I can say is that sometimes the world stops me cold

00:02:40.739 --> 00:02:43.589
And I feel my smallness and you'd think that would be sad

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:50.339
But it isn't it only makes me grateful Toni Morrison once wrote that at some point in life the world's beauty becomes enough

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:55.049
You don't need to photograph paint or even remember it it is enough

00:02:55.049 --> 00:02:58.769
So I turned off my camera, and it was it was enough

00:02:59.319 --> 00:03:01.319
Hank I'll see you on Friday

